    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "In the past several chapters, numerous methods for the estimation of variance components were covered. All of these methods are based on the same principle — that the expected phenotypic covariances between various kinds of relatives can be expressed as linear functions of causal components of genetic, and in some cases environmental, variance. These principles extend naturally to the estimation of causal components of phenotypic covariance, except that instead of comparing the same trait in two relatives, two different characters are compared — one in each relative. The expected phenotypic covariance of character 1 in individual x and character 2 in individual y follows naturally from the formulations of Cockerham (1954) and Kempthorne (1954):",
      "source_page": 643,
      "printed_page": 623,
      "source_block_ids": [
